<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">British Java</h1><p class="st-meta">1811 CE – 1816 CE · capital Batavia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Five-year British rule of Java during the Napoleonic Wars, remembered for Raffles, land-tax reform and the sack of Yogyakarta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> targeted its colonies. In August 1811 a force from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-elliot-1st-earl-of-minto">Lord Minto</a> and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-auchmuty">Samuel Auchmuty</a> landed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> and defeated the Franco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> army of Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-willem-janssens">Jan Willem Janssens</a>, who capitulated in September. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Thomas Stamford Raffles</a> was appointed lieutenant-governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Raffles</a> replaced forced deliveries with a land rent assessed on villages, reduced the powers of Javanese regents, and in 1812 stormed the kraton of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yogyakarta-sultanate">Yogyakarta Sultanate</a>, creating <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pakualaman">Pakualaman</a>. He also attacked the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palembang-sultanate">Palembang Sultanate</a>. British officials surveyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and Prambanan, and Raffles wrote his History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1814">Anglo-Dutch Convention of 1814</a> returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and the British handed over in August 1816, when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> was restored. Some of the land-rent reforms survived, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Raffles</a> went on to found <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-java.jpg" alt="British Java" width="320" height="207"><figcaption>Image: William John Huggins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Boats_of_H.M.S_Sloop_Procris_(10_guns)_engaging_French_Gunboats_off_the_mouth_of_the_Indramayo,_Java.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1811 CE – 1816 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Batavia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thomas Stamford Raffles, John Fendall</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (local)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese, Malay, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Indonesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British occupation of Java, British interregnum in the Dutch East Indies</dd></dl></aside></div>
